#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:2
msgid "System usernames"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:4
msgid ""
"System usernames can be used by snap developers to enable them to {ref}"
"`control services <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-control-services>` as a user "
"other than the default `root`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:6
msgid ""
"Outside of snaps, applications traditionally adopt the concept of users and "
"groups from the host operating system to use as a security mechanism that "
"grants access to specific system and software resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:8
msgid ""
"Snap confinement prohibits a system's users and groups from being used in "
"this way within a snap but a *snap_daemon* user and group can alternatively "
"be created within a snap to provide similar user and group level control "
"outside of a snap's confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:10
msgid "snap_daemon user and group"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:12
msgid ""
"**From snapd 2.61 onwards, `snap_daemon` is being deprecated and replaced "
"with `_daemon_` (with underscores), which now possesses a UID of `584792`.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:14
msgid ""
"To create the `snap_daemon` user/group inside a snap, add the following "
"`system-usernames` section to the snap's snapcraft.yaml:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:16
msgid ""
"system-usernames:\n"
"  snap_daemon: shared\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:21
msgid ""
"With the above added, the `584788` user and group can be used by a snap via "
"standard APIs (eg, `getpwent()`, the `getent` command, etc) to perform "
"various ownership (eg, `chown()` and `chgrp()`) and privilege separation "
"operations (eg, `setuid()` and `setgid()`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:23
msgid "Usage considerations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:25
msgid ""
"In general, the snap may use the allocated system username as desired. "
"However, due to snapd’s security sandbox, the snap may need to be adjusted "
"to work reliably."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:27
msgid ""
"Using `system-usernames` allows a snap to drop privileges and transition to "
"the specified user, but the daemon will still start as root and be started "
"from systemd on boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:29
msgid "Dropping privileges"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:31
msgid ""
"Security best practice dictates that applications drop privileges as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:33
msgid ""
"// Error messages, etc., omitted for clarity\n"
"char *user = \"snap_daemon\";\n"
"struct passwd *pwd = getpwnam(user);\n"
"if (pwd == NULL) {\n"
"    exit(1);\n"
"}\n"
"if (setgroups(0, NULL) < 0) {\n"
"    exit(1);\n"
"}\n"
"if (setgid(pwd->pw_gid) < 0) {\n"
"    exit(1);\n"
"}\n"
"if (setuid(pwd->pw_uid) < 0) {\n"
"    exit(1);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:51
msgid ""
"The observant reader will notice that the call to `setgroups()` uses the non-"
"portable, Linux-specific, invocation  _size 0_ and _NULL list_ to clear the "
"list completely. This contrasts with a more portable _size 1_ and a list "
"consisting of the single primary group. This is due to current sandbox "
"limitations. If desired, `setgroups()` may be overridden via LD_PRELOAD. See "
"the [test-setgroups snap](https://git.launchpad.net/~jdstrand/+git/test-"
"setgroups/tree/) for an example)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:53
msgid ""
"For those using `base: core18` or higher, the `setpriv` command may be used "
"to drop privileges like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:55
msgid ""
"$SNAP/usr/bin/setpriv --clear-groups --reuid snap_daemon \\\n"
"  --regid snap_daemon -- <your command>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:60
msgid ""
"`base: core18` snaps should stage `setpriv` while `base: core20` should "
"stage `util-linux`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:62
msgid ""
"LD_PRELOAD=$SNAP_COMMON/wraplib.so $SNAP/usr/bin/setpriv \\\n"
"  --clear-groups --reuid snap_daemon --regid snap_daemon -- <your command>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:67
msgid ""
"Some applications may try to drop privileges themselves using `initgroups()` "
"which under the hood uses `setgroups()` in a way that is blocked by the "
"sandbox. In this case, the snap would need to use the LD_PRELOAD method. See "
"the [test-setpriv snap](https://git.launchpad.net/~jdstrand/+git/test-"
"setpriv/tree/) for how to use `setpriv` with and without LD_PRELOAD."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:70
msgid "Process management via signals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:72
msgid ""
"By default, a snap's security sandbox limits the sending and receiving of "
"signals to processes in the snap where the sender and the receiver have the "
"same owner, even when the process runs as root (unless the process has "
"CAP_KILL)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:74
msgid ""
"When developing a snap, care must therefore be taken to drop privileges "
"_before_ sending a signal to a process that has privilege dropped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:76
msgid ""
"For example, a management process that runs as root may fork off worker "
"processes that drop privileges to the snap_daemon user. Whenever this "
"management process wants to send a signal to its workers, it must drop "
"privileges to the snap_daemon user first. Alternatively, the snap could use "
"`plugs: [ process-control ]`, which among other things, grants CAP_KILL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:78
msgid ""
"Since the {ref}`process-control <interfaces-process-control-interface>` "
"interface grants considerable access for system-wide process management, "
"best practice dictates that privileges _must_ be dropped as needed when "
"sending signals to other processes in the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:80
msgid "Ownership (discretionary access controls)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:82
msgid ""
"The security sandbox generally limits file access to where the object id of "
"the file (ie, the owner) matches the uid of the running process. This is "
"true even for root, unless the process has CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE or "
"CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH defined, which the security sandbox intentionally denies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:84
msgid ""
"As such, care must be taken when creating files after dropping privileges "
"_if_ those files are intended to be accessed by other processes in the snap "
"that have not had their privileges dropped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:86
msgid ""
"While each snap's requirements may differ, in general, a reasonable approach "
"is to create files and directories with `<dropped user>:root` ownership, "
"allowing owner and group read and write. This allows allows both the "
"_privilege dropped_ user and the _root_ user within the snap to access to "
"the files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:88
msgid ""
"For example, if the snap utilises the `snap_daemon` user, the snap might (as "
"part of a configure hook or a wrapper script, for instance) do:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:90
msgid ""
"if [ ! -d \"$SNAP_DATA/dir\" ]; then\n"
"    mkdir \"$SNAP_DATA/dir\"\n"
"    chmod 770 \"$SNAP_DATA/dir\" # must be before chown\n"
"    chown snap_daemon \"$SNAP_DATA/dir\"\n"
"    chgrp root \"$SNAP_DATA/dir\"  # not needed but shown for clarity\n"
"fi\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:99
msgid ""
"Security best practice dictates file access should be performed with the "
"minimal privileges necessarily, so the snap is of course free to privilege-"
"drop prior to accessing the file instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:101
msgid "subuid, subgid and other container technologies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:103
msgid ""
"Snapd takes great care to avoid overlapping with other container "
"technologies (or in the case of systemd, working with [`systemd-nspawn`'s "
"collision detection](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/master/docs/"
"UIDS-GIDS.md)). It uses the `524288-589823` UID/GID range, for instance, to "
"help avoid the default ranges for LXD, Docker and other container systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/snap-development/system-usernames.md:105
msgid ""
"Some administrators may adjust their non-snap container runtimes to use non-"
"default values (eg, via `/etc/subuid`, `/etc/subgid`, etc). While it is non-"
"fatal for other container ranges to overlap with snapd's range, best "
"practice dictates that a different range should always be used to ensure a "
"clean separation between snapd and other container ranges in the kernel on "
"the system."
msgstr ""

